<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles III William, Margrave of Baden-Durlach</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1679">1679</a>–<a href="/years/1738">1738</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Charles III William · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-zahringen">House of Zähringen</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles III William was Margrave of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a> from 1709. In 1715 he founded the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a> as his new residence, laid out with streets radiating from the palace tower like the ribs of a fan. He offered religious toleration and privileges to attract settlers, and Karlsruhe later became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-iii-william-margrave-of-baden-durlach.jpg" alt="Charles III William, Margrave of Baden-Durlach" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Rudolf Huber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Huber_Carl_III.Wilhelm.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles William was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a>, the son of Margrave Frederick Magnus of Baden-Durlach and Augusta Maria of Schleswig-Holstein-Gottorp. His older brother had died aged about one month, so he was born hereditary prince. After studies in Utrecht, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lausanne">Lausanne</a> he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and began his military career in the Imperial Army in the summer of 1694, serving under his uncle Margrave Louis William of Baden-Baden, known as Türkenlouis.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> he was a senior officer from 1702 to 1709. He was wounded at the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a> in 1702, fought at Friedlingen that year, helped cover the retreat at Höchstädt in 1703 and fought under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-eugene-of-savoy">Prince Eugene of Savoy</a> at Blenheim in 1704, where he narrowly escaped death. He was promoted Generalfeldzeugmeister in 1705 and in 1715 imperial Generalfeldmarschall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>When he succeeded in 1709 his territory measured about 75 square kilometres and held about 70,000 people, with only two significant towns, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pforzheim">Pforzheim</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a>. The Estates had been removed from power in 1668, and the war left the administration in poor order. Charles William therefore met little resistance in centring government on himself. He held a weekly audience open to all subjects and issued regulations in 1709 and 1710 demanding absolute obedience from officials, and he fought corruption with harsh penalties, needing five regulations against bribery during his reign.</p><p>He inherited a heavily indebted country and followed the mercantilist theories of his time, introducing monopolies on iron, salt and tobacco. A rigorous fiscal programme began in 1732, and he is credited with reducing the debt by about 1 million florins in the first fifteen years of his reign, laying the basis for the reforms of his grandson Charles Frederick. He also kept a large garden: a catalogue of 1713 lists 2,121 varieties of flowers, 1,163 of them tulips, and he left at least 6,000 watercolours of his plants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dissatisfied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a>, he had the foundation stone of a new palace laid in the Hardtwald forest on 17 June 1715. Thirty-two avenues radiated from its central Lead Tower, a plan that still shapes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>. A decree of 24 September 1715 granted settlers legal, religious and financial privileges, the court moved by 1718, and the town had 2,000 inhabitants in 1719. Its wooden houses, painted red, earned it the name Red City.</p><p>Charles William died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a> in 1738, reportedly of a stroke while working in his tulip bed. His son Frederick had died in 1732, so his grandson Charles Frederick, aged ten, succeeded him under a regency.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Charles III William” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Charles III William</b> of Baden-Durlach founded Karlsruhe in 1715, a planned city radiating from the palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-baden">Margraviate of Baden</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Charles III William</b> founded Karlsruhe in 1715 as a new residence laid out in a fan around the palace.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-baden-durlach">Margraviate of Baden-Durlach</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durlach">Durlach</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49,8.47&amp;year=1717" aria-label="Durlach on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4746 454 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Charles III William, Margrave of Baden-Durlach"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5196 724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5196 724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49,8.47&amp;year=1717">What was here in 1717? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
